        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "a2f39542-4143-5fdf-a28d-764490500e68",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Right On!, Castle of Frankenstein, Monsters and Heroes, Galaxy Science Fiction, If, Creepy, Eerie, T.H.U.N.D.E.R. Agents\n- People: Larry Ivie, Calvin Thomas Beck\n- Companies: Gothic Castle Publishing Company, Kable News, Marvel Comics\n- Concepts: African-American celebrities, horror, science fiction, fantasy film magazines, superhero comics\n\nFacts:\n- Castle of Frankenstein was an American horror, science fiction, and fantasy film magazine published from 1962 to 1975 by Calvin Thomas Beck's Gothic Castle Publishing Company and distributed by Kable News.\n- In the mid-to-late 1960s, Larry Ivie provided painted covers and other editorial material for early issues of Castle of Frankenstein.\n- In the mid-to-late 1960s, Larry Ivie self-published seven issues of the newsstand magazine Monsters and Heroes and drew comic stories in it featuring his superhero Altron Boy.\n- Larry Ivie had artwork published in Galaxy Science Fiction and If, co-created the comic book T.H.U.N.D.E.R. Agents, and wrote stories for Marvel Comics, Creepy, and Eerie.\n- Right On! continued publishing until about 2011 and focused on African-American celebrities.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "c1844fd9-102a-5927-a6f2-870abe45f419",
        "text": "Page: Right On!. Sentence: It continued publishing to c. 2011 and focused on African-American celebrities.\nPage: Castle of Frankenstein. Sentence: Castle of Frankenstein was an American horror, science fiction and fantasy film magazine, published between 1962 and 1975 by Calvin Thomas Beck's Gothic Castle Publishing Company, distributed by Kable News.\nPage: Larry Ivie. Sentence: He provided painted covers and other editorial material for early issues of \"Castle of Frankenstein\" magazine, then self-published the seven issues of his own newsstand magazine \"Monsters and Heroes\", for which he drew comic stories of his own superhero Altron Boy, in the mid-to-late '60s; had his art published in the magazines \"Galaxy Science Fiction\" and \"If\", co-created the comic book \"T.H.U.N.D.E.R. Agents\", and wrote several stories for Marvel Comics and the horror magazines \"Creepy\" and \"Eerie\".",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
